  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">With the introduction of the new country planning process for the UN, Bangladesh Employers&apos; Federation (BEF) saw an opportunity to unify the voice of the private sector to contribute to the 2022 UNSDCF and engage with the UN Country Team on a systematic and integrated basis.

To reinforce the UNSDCF engagement with the support of members and other business associations, the BEF organized a two-day consultation/training programme in June 2021. Based on inputs collected, BEF formulated a Strategy for Enhanced Engagement of BEF/Employers&apos; in UN Country Programming containing the action and strategies to utilize entry points for private sector interventions, including in the framework of the 2022-25 DWCP, which will derive from the UNSDCF. The Strategy provides a structured approach for Bangladesh&apos;s business representative organizations to engage in the UNSDCF through a Private Sector Task Force to guide private sector engagement strategy; Preparation of a periodic &quot;Situation and Trends Analysis&quot; report linked to the review and update of the CCA and ISERP; and Preparation of an annual progress and lessons analysis report linked to UNSDCF and DWCP progress reporting. To develop the Strategy, the BEF strengthened its partnership with the Federation of Bangladesh Chambers of Commerce and Industry (FBCCI) and led the consolidation of views of the organized private sector. The partners agreed to address a range of developmental challenges, including poverty reduction, women&apos;s empowerment, climate change, impact of the pandemic among others, to be presented to the UN Common Country Analysis and the Immediate Socio-Economic Response Plan (ISERP) &#x2013; an important step to consolidate a common business agenda for the UNSDCF. 
The Strategy was discussed and endorsed by the BEF and FBCCI, which collectively represent more than 500 employer, business and trade associations.  BEF and FBCCI presented the strategy to the UNRC to discuss specific engagement modalities in Dec 2021. ILO conducted the awareness training/consultation workshop for BEF, its members and affiliates Business Associations on 7th and 8th June 2021. Thereafter, based on the views expressed by the employer participants at this workshop and the what has taken place hitherto in the UNDSCF process in Bangladesh, ILO supported a formulation of an Analytical Report focussing on a) Mapping of the linkages between Bangladesh&apos;s DWCP, CCA, ISERP, UNSDCF, and the DWCP and b) BEF&apos;s current and recommended engagement in the above processes. 

Finally, ILO supported BEF in drawing up an independent Strategy document that outlines the potential entry-points and BEF&apos;s optimal contributions to Bangladesh&apos;s UNSDCF (2021-2025) and the DWCP (2022-2025). ILO&apos;s provided technical support to facilitate consultations with partner organizations and members which informed the strategy paper.</narrative>
   </description>
